## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical, beginner-friendly plan for per-tenant log growth control without code changes; emphasizes safe rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Filesystem quotas
- logrotate
- tmpfiles.d cleanup
- Rollback strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple per-container log dir monitor (mock)
const {execSync} = require('child_process');
const threshold = 500*1024*1024;
const containers = ['ct1','ct2','ct3'];
containers.forEach(ct=>{
  const dir = `/var/log/${ct}`;
  try {
    const out = execSync(`du -sb ${dir}`).toString().trim();
    const size = parseInt(out.split('\t')[0],10);
    if(size>threshold) console.log(`${ct} over limit: ${size}`);
  } catch(e){}
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the rollback plan in a staging environment?
- What metrics would you collect to verify no regression after changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design advanced observability with safe overhead, tenant isolation, and rollback.

## Key Concepts
- eBPF tracepoints, container lifecycle, per-tenant tagging, tail-latency analysis, rollout and rollback.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-eBPF sketch: tag events with tenant and record startup latency
tracepoint("container_start", (ctx) => {
  const tenant = getCurrentTenantId();
  recordLatency(tenant, now() - startTime);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate that tracing does not perturb startup times under load?
- What metrics and dashboards would you expose to operators?

